---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
const oses = [
  ['Windows 10 / 11', 'ipconfig /flushdns', 'Command Prompt or PowerShell, run as Administrator is not required.'],
  ['macOS (modern)', 'sudo dscacheutil -flushcache; sudo killall -HUP mDNSResponder', 'One line, both parts. Sudo required.'],
  ['Linux (systemd)', 'sudo resolvectl flush-caches', 'Default on Ubuntu/Debian/Fedora today.'],
  ['Linux (nscd)', 'sudo service nscd restart', 'Older servers using nscd.'],
  ['Linux (dnsmasq)', 'sudo systemctl restart dnsmasq', 'Small-server default.'],
  ['Chrome (any OS)', 'chrome://net-internals/#dns → Clear host cache', 'Also clear sockets at chrome://net-internals/#sockets.'],
];

---
<BaseLayout title="How to Flush DNS Cache (Windows, macOS, Linux, Chrome)" description="Flush your DNS cache on every platform: Windows ipconfig /flushdns, macOS dscacheutil, Linux systemd-resolved, and Chrome's internal cache."  
 breadcrumb={[["Home", "/"], ["DNS Guide", "/dns/"], ["Flush DNS Cache"]]}>
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-12">
    <nav class="mb-4 text-sm text-ink-faint" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/" class="hover:text-accent">Home</a>
       <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <a href="/dns/" class="hover:text-accent">DNS Guide</a>
       <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <span class="text-ink-dim">Flush DNS Cache</span>
    </nav>
    <h1 class="font-display text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl">How to Flush DNS Cache</h1>
    <div class="pd-answer" data-vs="answer">
      <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">
      Changed a DNS record and still seeing the old site? Your own computer is often the last cache to
      let go. Here is the flush command for every platform.
    </p>
    </div>

    <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink">Flush commands</h2>
    <section class="not-prose my-4" aria-label="Reference table">
      <div class="glass rounded-2xl p-5 shadow-lg sm:p-7 overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="pdns-table" style="width:100%">
          <thead><tr><th>Platform</th><th>Command</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody><tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">Windows</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">ipconfig /flushdns</td></tr><tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">macOS</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">sudo dscacheutil -flushcache</td></tr><tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">Linux (systemd)</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">resolvectl flush-caches</td></tr><tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">Chrome</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">chrome://net-internals/#dns</td></tr></tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="mt-8 grid gap-3">
      {oses.map(([os, cmd, note]) => (
        <div class="rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 p-4">
          <p class="font-semibold text-ink">{os}</p>
          <pre class="mt-2 overflow-x-auto rounded-lg border border-line bg-panel-2 p-3 font-mono text-xs text-accent-soft">{cmd}</pre>
          <p class="mt-1.5 text-xs text-ink-faint">{note}</p>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>

    <div class="rich-text mt-10">
      <h2>When flushing helps (and when it doesn't)</h2>
      <p>
        Flushing clears <em>your device's</em> cache. If the old record is still cached at your ISP's
        resolver or a public resolver, flushing locally won't help, that cache expires on its TTL
        schedule. Verify what resolvers currently see with the
        <a href="/dns-propagation-checker/">propagation checker</a>: if the world sees the new value and
        you don't, flush. If nobody sees it yet, wait.
      </p>
      
<h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink">Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <div class="mt-3 grid gap-3"><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">How often should I flush DNS?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">Only when diagnosing or right after a record change. Routine flushing wastes time because the cache self-expires at each record TTL.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Does flushing DNS delete history?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">No. It clears only cached name resolutions, not browsing history, cookies, or saved passwords.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Why do websites still fail after flushing?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">The failure is probably not caching: verify the domain resolves with a record lookup, then check the resolver itself with the propagation checker.</p></details></div>
      <h2>Related</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="/dns-propagation/">DNS propagation explained</a></li>
        <li><a href="/site-down-after-dns-change/">Site down after a DNS change?</a></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
